unusually warm  and the impact of that increasingly  clear  we're getting more and more used to  seeing pictures like this  all tangible examples of our  ever-changing climate  and experts say it's only going to get  worse  and this is why the state of the climate  report now in its 31st edition reveals  how in recent years temperatures in  europe have been on the rise and in 2020  have reached 1.9 degrees above the  long-term average if we look at what  that's felt like in individual countries  many places particularly eastern europe  reported record high temperatures last  year russia was nearly three degrees  warmer than the average high that was  recorded between 1981 and 2010.  parts of western europe were relatively  cooler but norway nearly two degrees  above average and the uk we've had our  third warmest year since national  records began and those soaring  temperatures have also contributed to  rising sea levels with 2020 seeing the  record high for the ninth year in a row  sea levels rose more than nine  centimeters above the average back in  1993 that's due to warming oceans  melting glaciers and ice sheets so why  are temperatures going up well it's down  to record amounts of greenhouse gas in  the atmosphere peaking in 2020 in fact  the average concentration of co2 was up  48  on pre-industrial times and atmospheric  methane increased  160 percent during the same period and  this is the really difficult thing those  increases happened despite human co2  emissions decreasing by up to seven  percent during the pandemic so those  small but not insignificant changes made  no tangible difference to our  atmospheric co2 levels  these other emissions are going to come  from  things like power stations where they  are based on fossil fuels so coal and  natural gas we there were still some  flights for transporting goods and posts  for example so there are all kinds of  other emissions that we don't  necessarily see in our day-to-day lives  but are were still going on during 2020.  so that's the challenge how to hit our  global warming reduction targets and  scientists say the situation should  worry us all harriet hatfield sky news
 

